[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172130,
      "id": "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632",
      "title": "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d9ba2e0-102f-5ae7-abb7-4c8e12292bfc",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x^{2} y^{2} z^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Assume $u\\geq0.$ What is the missing expression? Choose an integrand equal to the required expression as a function on $D$.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.$*",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=4 \\sqrt{2} u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x^{2} y^{2} z^{2})\\,{|}_{x=2 u^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(2 u^{2}\\cdot 2 u^{2})\\cdot v^{2}\\cdot v^{2} \\\\  & =4 u^{4} v^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4 \\sqrt{2} u 4 u^{4} v^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "24f470c7-3e68-5664-8116-49ea2082cafa",
